{
  "teamId": 138,
  "year": 2022,
  "narrative": "The 2022 St. Louis Cardinals entered the season with quiet optimism, and early signs were encouraging. Nolan Arenado announced his presence with a go-ahead home run in April, but the bullpen showed early cracks — most painfully when Giovanny Gallegos surrendered a two-run single that flipped a game in the Mets' favor. The Cardinals finished April at 11-9, a solid if unspectacular foundation, and May brought both brilliance and heartbreak in equal measure: Paul Goldschmidt launched a grand slam and delivered a clutch single, but Pete Alonso's two-run blast off Gallegos reminded St. Louis that no lead felt truly safe.\n\nJune was where the season teetered. Drew VerHagen was victimized for a three-run homer that cost the Cardinals dearly, and Ryan Helsley's struggles in the late innings became a recurring wound. Yet Tommy Edman provided the month's most electric moment — a two-run walk-off homer that briefly steadied the ship. The team limped to 15-14 in June and slid further to 11-13 in July, the bullpen bleeding at crucial moments. It looked, for a stretch, like the Cardinals might fade from contention entirely.\n\nThen came August — perhaps the most dominant single month by any team in recent Cardinals history. A scorching 22-7 record transformed the season, fueled by Tyler O'Neill's three-run blasts, Paul DeJong's improbable clutch double off Aaron Judge's glove in right field, Lars Nootbaar's electric triple, and a rotation that suddenly held leads. September sustained the surge at 15-11, punctuated by Tommy Edman's walk-off double, Nolan Arenado's run-scoring liner, a sentimental Albert Pujols home run as he chased history, and Brendan Donovan's grand slam. St. Louis had built genuine momentum heading into October.\n\nThe Wild Card round against Philadelphia proved to be a painful denouement. Bryce Harper and Jean Segura delivered the crushing blows across two games, and though Juan Yepez provided a brief jolt of hope with a two-run homer, the Cardinals could not sustain any rally. Albert Pujols grounded into a double play in what may have been his final at-bat in a Cardinals uniform, and the season ended with a whimper rather than the roar the August surge had promised. A 93-win season, a dominant summer, and a legend's farewell — all extinguished in the span of two October nights in Philadelphia.",
